{% comment %}
  The rebuilt site header. See docs/header-rebuild.md for the decisions behind
  it; the short version:

  ONE SECTION, BOTH LAYOUTS. This is written to be rendered by suf.liquid AND
  theme.liquid, which is why nothing here depends on `.suf-body`. Everything it
  needs it emits itself: the scope class is `.sufnav`, on the root element.

  THE CART DRAWER IS OUT OF SCOPE. The cart icon is a link to /cart and a
  count, and nothing else. That is already the behaviour on both layouts, so
  pointing theme.liquid at this section changes nothing about the drawer --
  legacy product pages keep opening it from their own add-to-cart button.

  NEW MARKUP, not snippets/navigation.liquid. The dt-sc-* vocabulary and its
  mega-menu scaffolding are gone; suf-navlabel.liquid is called directly from
  here instead.

  ACCOUNT LINKS come from `routes`, never a literal URL. This store has new
  customer accounts enabled and routes.account_login_url already resolves to
  the /customer_authentication/redirect form with the right locale and region.

  THE MENU SETTING HAS NO DEFAULT, deliberately. Shopify accepts only its own
  reserved handles -- 'main-menu' and 'footer' -- as a link_list default, and
  rejects the entire schema for anything else. This shop's menu is
  'main-menu-redesign', so it is seeded in config/settings_data.json instead.
  ('comment' is not an allowed schema property either, which is why this note
  is here rather than beside the setting.)

  The parent of a submenu is a <button>, not a link. It opens a panel; it does
  not go anywhere, and on touch there is no hover to open it with. Its own
  destination, if the menu item has one, is repeated as the first row of the
  panel so it is still reachable.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign menu = linklists[section.settings.menu]
  assign classes = 'sufnav'
  if section.settings.sticky
    assign classes = classes | append: ' sufnav--sticky'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The sentinel is what tells the header it has scrolled: an IntersectionObserver
  watches it leave the viewport. A scroll listener would fire on every frame to
  answer the same yes/no question.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="sufnav__sentinel" data-sufnav-sentinel aria-hidden="true"></div>

<div class="sufnav-wrap">
  <header class="{{ classes }}" data-sufnav>
    <a class="sufnav__logo" href="{{ routes.root_url }}">
      {%- if section.settings.logo -%}
        {%- assign logo_alt = section.settings.logo.alt | default: shop.name -%}
        {{
          section.settings.logo
          | image_url: width: 480
          | image_tag: alt: logo_alt, loading: 'eager', fetchpriority: 'high', widths: '160,240,320,480'
        }}
      {%- else -%}
        <span class="sufnav__logo-text">{{ shop.name }}</span>
      {%- endif -%}
    </a>

    <nav class="sufnav__menu" id="sufnav-menu" aria-label="Main">
      {%- for link in menu.links -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign item_classes = 'sufnav__item'
          if link.links.size > 0
            assign item_classes = item_classes | append: ' sufnav__item--has'
          endif

          # PATHS ARE COMPARED HERE RATHER THAN TRUSTING link.active.
          #
          # link.active matches a menu link's URL against the current page, and
          # these menu links carry a ?view= preview parameter -- the technique
          # in docs/migration.md for rendering a new template without
          # reassigning it in the admin. A custom URL with a query string never
          # matches a clean path, so link.active is false on every suf page.
          # /blogs/blog has no parameter, which is why the blog was the one
          # item that ever highlighted.
          #
          # Splitting on ? and comparing to request.path is true in BOTH
          # states, so this keeps working after cutover drops the parameters
          # rather than quietly starting to work then.
          assign here = false
          assign link_path = link.url | split: '?' | first
          if link.active or link_path == request.path
            assign here = true
          endif

          # A parent is also "here" when one of its children is.
          unless here
            for child in link.links
              assign child_path = child.url | split: '?' | first
              if child.active or child_path == request.path
                assign here = true
                break
              endif
            endfor
          endunless

          if here or link.child_active
            assign item_classes = item_classes | append: ' is-active'
          endif
        -%}
        <div class="{{ item_classes }}">
          {%- if link.links.size > 0 -%}
            <button
              class="sufnav__label"
              type="button"
              aria-expanded="false"
              aria-controls="sufnav-drop-{{ forloop.index }}"
            >
              {% render 'suf-navlabel', title: link.title %}
              <span class="sufnav__caret" aria-hidden="true">
                {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'chevron-down', size: 12 %}
              </span>
            </button>

            {%- comment -%}
              NO REPEATED PARENT ROW. The disclosure pattern normally opens the
              panel with the parent's own destination, since the parent is a
              button and no longer navigates. Here it earns nothing: the
              category parents are labels rather than pages, and the ones that
              do have a page already list it among their children -- so the row
              was a visible duplicate.

              THE TRAP, if a menu item is added later: a parent with a real URL
              that is NOT among its own children becomes unreachable from the
              header, because the button only opens the panel. Add it as the
              first child in the Shopify menu editor.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <div class="sufnav__drop" id="sufnav-drop-{{ forloop.index }}">
              {%- for child in link.links -%}
                {%- assign child_path = child.url | split: '?' | first -%}
                <a
                  href="{{ child.url }}"
                  {% if child.active or child_path == request.path %}
                    aria-current="page"
                  {% endif %}
                >
                  {% render 'suf-navlabel', title: child.title %}
                </a>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </div>
          {%- else -%}
            <a
              class="sufnav__label"
              href="{{ link.url }}"
              {% if here %}
                aria-current="page"
              {% endif %}
            >
              {% render 'suf-navlabel', title: link.title %}
            </a>
          {%- endif -%}
        </div>
      {%- endfor -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The account row: mobile only, inside the panel. Its desktop counterpart
        is the icon below. Logging out is a POST -- new customer accounts will
        not accept a GET -- so it is a form, not a link.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if customer -%}
        <form method="post" action="{{ routes.account_logout_url }}" class="sufnav__acct">
          {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'user' %}
          <button type="submit">Log Out</button>
        </form>
      {%- else -%}
        <a class="sufnav__acct" href="{{ routes.account_login_url }}">
          {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'user' %}
          <span>Log In</span>
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </nav>

    <div class="sufnav__icons">
      <a
        class="sufnav__icon sufnav__icon--acct"
        href="{% if customer %}{{ routes.account_url }}{% else %}{{ routes.account_login_url }}{% endif %}"
      >
        {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'user', label: 'Account' %}
      </a>

      {%- comment -%}
        Always /cart. The badge is server-rendered from cart.item_count, so it
        is correct on first paint and needs no JavaScript.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="sufnav__icon sufnav__icon--cart" href="{{ routes.cart_url }}">
        {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'cart', label: 'Cart' %}
        {%- comment -%}
          EMPTY, NOT hidden, when the cart is. On legacy templates
          assets/ajax-cart.js.liquid writes the new count into #CartCount when
          the drawer updates -- it would set the text and leave a `hidden`
          attribute it knows nothing about in place, so a first item added
          through the drawer would update an invisible badge.

          Rendering nothing instead lets `.sufnav__badge:empty` do the hiding,
          and the badge reappears by itself the moment anything writes a number
          into it.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        <span class="sufnav__badge" id="CartCount">
          {%- if cart.item_count > 0 -%}{{ cart.item_count }}{%- endif -%}
        </span>
      </a>

      <button
        class="sufnav__icon sufnav__icon--search"
        type="button"
        data-suf-search-toggle
        aria-expanded="false"
      >
        {% render 'suf-icon', icon: 'search', label: 'Search' %}
      </button>
    </div>

    <button
      class="sufnav__burger"
      type="button"
      data-sufnav-burger
      aria-expanded="false"
      aria-controls="sufnav-menu"
      aria-label="Menu"
    >
      <span></span><span></span><span></span>
    </button>
  </header>

  {%- comment -%}
    Inside the wrapper, below the bar, so opening it pushes the page down
    rather than covering it -- and so it travels with the header when sticky.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {% render 'suf-search' %}
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Header (SUF)",
  "class": "sufnav-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "link_list",
      "id": "menu",
      "label": "Menu",
      "info": "Add ' | subtext' to a menu item's title to give it a second line in the dropdown."
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "logo",
      "label": "Logo",
      "info": "White or light artwork -- the bar is navy."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "sticky",
      "label": "Stick to the top on scroll",
      "info": "The bar compresses once the page scrolls.",
      "default": true
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
